WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:05.200
Poor people need it, rich people keep it,
and only few can resist it.

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:07.549
It can make us happy, but not always.

00:00:07.549 --> 00:00:12.380
Intrinsically it has zero value, but then
it’s maybe the only thing in this world

00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:14.070
we all agree to trust.

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:20.130
To see how money came about, it helps to understand
its history.

00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:27.470
First, there was barter, a system in which
people simply exchanged what they wanted with

00:00:27.470 --> 00:00:28.820
what they had.

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:33.590
This simple trade of goods led to an immense
increase in specialization, economic growth

00:00:33.590 --> 00:00:34.590
and wealth.

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:39.460
To find each other and exchange what they
had for what they needed, people formed markets.

00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:43.730
To ensure that their produce can be trusted,
they later invented brands.

00:00:43.730 --> 00:00:47.309
But there was a problem.

00:00:47.309 --> 00:00:51.489
Barter is based on a "double coincidence of
wants", which means that people who wanted

00:00:51.489 --> 00:00:54.460
each other's stuff, also had to find another.

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:58.320
In a world without forms of telecommunications
this wasn’t easy.

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:02.500
And as a result they often had to go an extra
mile to find their luck.

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:07.479
Around 3,500 years ago someone came up with
the idea to weigh products in the form of

00:01:07.479 --> 00:01:13.850
rare metals and gold and silver became a medium
of exchange.

00:01:13.850 --> 00:01:18.170
This was possible because there was a shared
belief that gold was as beautiful as it was

00:01:18.170 --> 00:01:19.220
scarce.

00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:23.630
Gold allowed us to replace barter, and make
and sell things, and then save the income

00:01:23.630 --> 00:01:24.630
of our earning.

00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:27.280
The ability to store value was born.

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:31.380
This meant we were able to take the earnings
of our work into the future, which led to

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:35.180
an immense increase in innovation and specialization.

00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:41.140
Trade exploded and as a result, a lot of wealth
was created.

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:46.909
Around 1,000 years ago, some smart businessmen
realized that trust itself was as good as

00:01:46.909 --> 00:01:47.909
gold.

00:01:47.909 --> 00:01:51.420
In exchange for goods, they simply promised
the seller to give them the gold later and

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:53.770
handed them a letter of credit instead..

00:01:53.770 --> 00:01:58.329
Paper notes had the advantage that they were
lighter than gold, and easier to count, store

00:01:58.329 --> 00:01:59.680
and protect.

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:03.869
Rulers and politicians then soon realized
that they can issue these notes themselves

00:02:03.869 --> 00:02:06.219
and started prohibiting others to do so.

00:02:06.219 --> 00:02:10.119
To win the trust of their people, they promised
to pay back every note with an equivalent

00:02:10.119 --> 00:02:13.480
amount of gold.

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:18.239
Around 100 years ago rulers began craving
for more money than they had, and so they

00:02:18.239 --> 00:02:22.760
stopped backing it with gold, and just began
printing as many notes as they needed — the

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:27.459
so-called fiat money was born.

00:02:27.459 --> 00:02:31.830
The newly printed money was often given to
business owners in the form of cheap loans.

00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:35.519
This allowed them to invest, hire, and increase
economic activity.

00:02:35.519 --> 00:02:39.560
Employees who earned and saved that money
realized that while prices of everyday goods

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:43.380
went up over time, the buying power of their
savings went down.

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:50.590
As a result they often spend their savings
fast— further increasing economic activity.

00:02:50.590 --> 00:02:55.260
But soon some began to understand that while
their incomes remained relatively flat, housing

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:57.870
and company stocks kept rising and rising

00:02:57.870 --> 00:03:01.710
Inflation of asset prices happened because
while there was more and more money, there

00:03:01.710 --> 00:03:04.879
was still just the same limited amount of
land to buy.

00:03:04.879 --> 00:03:08.810
Some started to argue that whenever governments
print new money, they effectively reduce the

00:03:08.810 --> 00:03:13.420
savings of the poor and increase the prices
of homes, usually owned by the rich.

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:18.120
This is one reason people began searching
for a new form of money.

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:22.379
Cryptocurrencies, like Bitcoin, are the latest
form of money.

00:03:22.379 --> 00:03:25.620
They work just like the others because people
can place trust in them.

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:30.900
Unlike fiat money, bitcoins are created by
a decentralized network of mathematical code.

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:34.510
That means that they can be transferred directly
from person to person without the involvement

00:03:34.510 --> 00:03:39.920
of middlemen and no-one can increase the fixed
amount of 21 million bitcoins there are - which

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:44.689
is the main reason people trust and save them.

00:03:44.689 --> 00:03:45.689
What do you think?

00:03:45.689 --> 00:03:49.500
Will history end here and bitcoin become and
remain the last form of money?

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:54.370
And if bitcoins disappear again, which form
of money can we all agree to trust going forward?

00:03:54.370 --> 00:03:59.610
Before you make your argument, you may want
to know that so-called honest or good money

00:03:59.610 --> 00:04:02.050
usually has five characteristics:

00:04:02.050 --> 00:04:04.090
Scarcity
Durability

00:04:04.090 --> 00:04:06.340
Portability
Divisibility

